  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_013",
  "condition": "modernize",
  "rewritten_text": "Just yesterday I told Mr. Cole that I was embarrassed to look at our new grand piano in the drawing‑room, since I can't play a single note, and our little girls are only just starting and may never get anywhere with it; and poor Jane Fairfax, who is a music teacher, doesn't even have a tiny old spinet to practice on. I said this to Mr. Cole yesterday, and he completely agreed; but he's such a music lover that he couldn't resist buying it, hoping some of our good neighbours might be kind enough to use it better than we can, and that's really why we bought it—otherwise we'd be ashamed of it. We're hoping Miss Woodhouse will agree to try it this evening.\n\nMiss Woodhouse agreed, and seeing there was nothing more to get from Mrs. Cole, she turned to Frank Churchill. \"Why are you smiling?\" she asked. \"Why are you?\" he replied. \"Me? I suppose I'm smiling because Colonel Campbell is so rich and generous. It's a splendid gift.\" \"Indeed.\" \"I find it odd that it was never given before.\" \"Perhaps Miss Fairfax has never stayed here long enough.\" \"Or maybe he didn't let her use their own instrument, which is probably locked away in London, untouched.\" \"That's a grand piano, and he might think it's too big for Mrs. Bates's house.\" \"You can say what you like, but your expression shows you think the same as I do.\" \"I don't know. I think you're giving me more credit for insight than I deserve. I smile because you smile, and I probably suspect whatever you suspect; but right now I don't see anything to question. If Colonel Campbell isn't the one, who could be?\" \"What about Mrs. Dixon?\" \"Mrs. Dixon! That's true. I hadn't thought of her. She and her father would both understand how valuable an instrument would be, and perhaps the whole scheme feels more like a young woman's plan than an older man's. I think it's Mrs. Dixon. I told you your suspicions would guide mine.\" \"If that's the case, you need to include Mr. Dixon in your suspicions as well.\" \"Mr. Dixon—right. Yes, I immediately think it must be a joint gift from Mr. and Mrs. Dixon. We were just talking the other day about how much he admires her playing.\" \"Yes, and what you told me confirmed an idea I already had. I'm not trying to judge the good intentions of either Mr. Dixon or Miss Fairfax, but I can't help suspecting that after he proposed to her friend, he unfortunately fell in love with her, or that he sensed a small attachment on her part.\""
}

{
  "passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_019",
  "condition": "paraphrase",
  "rewritten_text": "Woodhouse, I wish you had heard the gallant compliments he paid me at dinner. I assure you I began to think my intended husband would be positively jealous. I fancy I am rather a favourite; he even remarked upon my gown. How do you like it?—Selina’s choice—handsome, I think, though I cannot tell whether it is not over‑adorned; I have a great aversion to the notion of being over‑adorned—quite a horror of finery. I must now add a few ornaments, because it is expected of me. A bride, you know, must appear like a bride, yet my natural taste leans toward simplicity; a plain style of dress is infinitely preferable to elaborate trimmings. But I am quite in the minority, I believe; few people seem to value simplicity of dress—show and finery are everything. I have some idea of adding a trimming such as this to my white and silver poplin. Do you think it will look well?\n\nThe whole party had only just reassembled in the drawing‑room when Mr. Weston made his entrance among them. He had returned from a late dinner and walked to Hartfield as soon as it was over. He had been much anticipated by the best judges, for surprise—but there was great joy. Mr. Woodhouse was almost as glad to see him now as he would have been sorry to have seen him earlier. John Knightley was left in mute astonishment. That a man who might have spent his evening quietly at home after a day of business in London should set off again, and walk half a mile to another man’s house, for the sake of being in mixed company till bedtime, of ending his day in the efforts of civility and the noise of numbers, struck him deeply. A man who had been in motion since eight o’clock in the morning, and might now have been still, who had been long talking, and might have been silent, who had been in more than one crowd, and might have been alone!—Such a man, to quit the tranquillity and independence of his own fireside, and on the evening of a cold, sleety April day rush out again into the world!—If he could by a touch of his finger have instantly taken back his wife, there would have been a motive; but his coming would probably prolong rather than break up the party. John Knightley looked at him with amazement, then shrugged his shoulders, and said, “I could not have believed it even of him.”\n\nMr. Weston, meanwhile, perfectly unaware of the indignation he was provoking, was happy and cheerful as usual, and, with all the right of being the principal talker—a privilege a day spent away from home confers—made himself agreeable among the rest; having satisfied his wife’s inquiries about his dinner, convincing her that none of her careful directions to the servants had been forgotten, and spreading the public news he had heard, he proceeded to a family communication which, though principally addressed to Mrs. Weston, he had no doubt would be of great interest to everyone in the room. He handed her a letter; it was from Frank, addressed to herself; he had met with it on his way and had taken the liberty of opening it."
}

